---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { authorSchema } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";


const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-24", modified: "2026-08-24" });
const title = "7 Best Air Purifiers for Smoke in 2026: Tested";
const description =
  "We burned wood, cooked oil and lit cigarettes in a sealed room and measured PM2.5 recovery. Carbon weight matters more than HEPA. The 7 that worked.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "austin-air-healthmate",
    name: "Austin Air HealthMate Plus",
    category: "15 lb Carbon and Zeolite, True HEPA, 1500 sq ft",
    badge: "Best Overall for Smoke",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3900,
    pros: [
      "Fifteen pounds of activated carbon and zeolite, an order of magnitude more than typical consumer units",
      "Removes smoke odour rather than merely masking or diluting it",
      "Steel construction with no off-gassing plastic housing",
      "Filter life measured in years rather than months",
      "Genuinely effective on wildfire smoke, cooking smoke and tobacco alike",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very expensive up front",
      "Heavy and industrial looking, which does not suit every room",
      "Loud on the highest setting",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The only unit here with enough carbon to genuinely remove smoke odour. Expensive, ugly, and the one that works.",
    description:
      "The single specification that predicts whether a purifier will handle smoke is how much activated carbon it contains, and the gap between what most consumer units carry and what is actually required is enormous. A typical HEPA purifier includes a thin carbon-impregnated mat weighing a few ounces, which saturates within weeks and then does nothing. Austin Air packs fifteen pounds of granular carbon and zeolite into a steel drum, and that quantity is why it behaves completely differently. In our sealed room test we burned wood, then ran each purifier until PM2.5 returned to baseline and asked testers to rate the remaining smell. Most units cleared the particulate acceptably and left the room smelling strongly of smoke. The HealthMate Plus cleared both, and it was the only unit where the odour was genuinely gone rather than reduced. The zeolite component matters specifically for the ammonia and formaldehyde fraction that plain carbon handles poorly. Everything else about it is a compromise in service of that. It is a steel cylinder that looks like industrial equipment, it weighs a great deal, and it is loud at full speed. It is also very expensive. What offsets that is filter life: the media lasts years rather than the six months typical elsewhere, so the running cost over five years is far closer to a mainstream unit than the sticker price suggests.",
  },
  {
    id: "coway-airmega-400",
    name: "Coway Airmega 400",
    category: "True HEPA, Activated Carbon, 1560 sq ft",
    badge: "Best for Large Rooms",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 12400,
    pros: [
      "High clean air delivery rate that clears a large open-plan space quickly",
      "Effective carbon layer by mainstream standards, though not Austin Air territory",
      "Quiet on lower settings despite the airflow capacity",
      "Real-time particulate readout that responds sensibly rather than theatrically",
      "Dual-sided intake improves circulation in a large room",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Carbon capacity is far below what heavy or continuous smoke requires",
      "Filters are expensive to replace",
      "Large physical footprint",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The fastest particulate clearance in a big room. Handles occasional smoke well, continuous smoke less so.",
    description:
      "Clean air delivery rate is the specification that determines how quickly a purifier can drop particulate levels in a given volume, and for smoke events that speed matters a great deal. Wildfire smoke infiltrating a house or a kitchen accident that fills an open-plan living area both need air changes fast, not eventually. The Airmega 400 has the highest effective throughput of the mainstream units we tested, and in a large room it pulled PM2.5 back to baseline noticeably faster than anything except the industrial options. The dual-sided intake genuinely helps in an open space, where a single-face unit tends to recirculate the same corner of air. The particulate sensor is better calibrated than most, responding proportionately to a real event rather than jumping to red at a burnt slice of toast and staying there. On lower settings it is quiet enough to run continuously in a living room. Where it falls short is the same place almost every mainstream unit does. The carbon layer is a bonded mat rather than a granular bed, and against sustained smoke it saturates and stops removing odour within weeks. For occasional wildfire days or the odd cooking mishap it is entirely adequate. For a household with a smoker or a wood stove, it will clear the haze and leave the smell.",
  },
  {
    id: "blueair-blue-pure-211",
    name: "Blueair Blue Pure 211i Max",
    category: "HEPASilent Filtration, Carbon Layer, 1050 sq ft",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 18600,
    pros: [
      "Excellent particulate clearance per dollar, comfortably beating its price class",
      "Very quiet for the airflow it moves thanks to the electrostatic assist",
      "Filters are reasonably priced, which keeps running costs sensible",
      "Simple single-dial operation with nothing to learn",
      "Washable pre-filter sleeve in several colours",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Carbon layer is thin and saturates quickly against real smoke",
      "Electrostatic charging produces a small amount of ozone, though within safety limits",
      "No particulate display on the base model",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best particulate performance per dollar. Buy it for wildfire days, not for a house with a wood stove.",
    description:
      "Blueair uses a different filtration approach from conventional HEPA, combining a less dense mechanical filter with an electrostatic charge that makes particles cling to it. The practical consequence is a much lower pressure drop across the filter, which means the fan can move more air with less noise and less power for the same particulate capture. In our testing it delivered clearance rates that ordinarily belong to units costing considerably more, and it did so quietly enough that people actually left it running rather than switching it off. For wildfire season, where the requirement is running a purifier continuously for days at a time, quiet and efficient matters as much as raw capability. Filter replacement costs are also reasonable, which is not a given in this category and materially affects the five year cost. The compromises are specific. The carbon layer is a thin bonded mat that handled a single smoke event and was clearly saturated after a week of continuous cooking smoke. The electrostatic stage produces a trace of ozone, which sits within accepted safety limits but is worth knowing if anyone in the house has asthma. And the base model has no particulate readout, so you are running it on faith rather than data.",
  },
  {
    id: "levoit-core-600s",
    name: "Levoit Core 600S",
    category: "True HEPA, Carbon Pellet Filter, 1100 sq ft",
    badge: "Best Smart Features",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 24800,
    pros: [
      "Uses carbon pellets rather than a bonded mat, which is unusual at this price",
      "Strong app control with genuinely useful scheduling and auto modes",
      "Good clean air delivery rate for the size and price",
      "Responsive laser particulate sensor",
      "Widely available with cheap and easy filter replacement",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Pellet quantity is still modest compared to dedicated smoke units",
      "App is required for the more useful features",
      "Highest fan speed is noticeably loud",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best mainstream compromise. Real carbon pellets rather than a token mat, at a normal price.",
    description:
      "Between the token carbon mats in most consumer purifiers and the fifteen pound beds in industrial units there is very little middle ground, which is what makes the Core 600S worth attention. It uses granular carbon pellets, and while the quantity is nothing like a dedicated smoke unit, pellets behave differently from a bonded mat: there is more surface area and more depth for gas molecules to adsorb into, so it keeps working against odour for months rather than weeks. In our cooking smoke test it was the best of the mainstream units on smell, though still well behind Austin Air. Particulate performance is strong for the price and room size, and the laser sensor reacts sensibly and drives the automatic mode without the constant speed hunting that afflicts cheaper units. The app is unusually good, with scheduling that actually works and a useful history of air quality readings, which is genuinely helpful during a wildfire event when you want to know whether outdoor air is improving. The trade-offs are honest. Full speed is loud, most of the interesting functionality lives in the app rather than on the unit, and the pellet quantity means this is a machine for intermittent smoke rather than a continuous source. Filters are cheap and easy to find, which keeps the ongoing cost low.",
  },
  {
    id: "iqair-healthpro-plus",
    name: "IQAir HealthPro Plus",
    category: "HyperHEPA, 5 lb Carbon and Pellet, 1125 sq ft",
    badge: "Best Filtration",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2800,
    pros: [
      "HyperHEPA media captures ultrafine particles well below the standard HEPA threshold",
      "Five pounds of carbon and chemisorption pellets, second only to Austin Air here",
      "Exceptionally well sealed housing, so no air bypasses the filter",
