  {
    id: "winix-5500-2",
    name: "Winix 5500-2",
    category: "True HEPA, Washable Carbon Filter, PlasmaWave, 360 sq ft",
    badge: "Best Budget",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 31200,
    pros: [
      "Very low purchase price for genuine true HEPA filtration",
      "Washable carbon pre-filter reduces ongoing costs considerably",
      "Good particulate clearance in a bedroom-sized space",
      "Auto mode with a functional particulate sensor",
      "Widely available with inexpensive replacement filters",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Carbon filter is thin and washing it does not restore adsorption capacity",
      "PlasmaWave ioniser should be switched off, and it defaults on",
      "Only suitable for a single room rather than an open plan space",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Excellent particulate value for a bedroom. Understand that the washable carbon filter does not really recover.",
    description:
      "For a single bedroom during wildfire season, the requirement is straightforward: pull fine particulate out of a modest volume of air, quietly, without spending a great deal. The Winix 5500-2 has been the default answer to that question for years, and it holds up. Particulate clearance in a bedroom-sized room was solid, the auto mode responds usefully, and the price is low enough that buying two for two bedrooms is realistic in a way that most units here are not. Two details deserve honesty. The washable carbon filter is presented as a running cost saving, and while rinsing it does remove dust and restore airflow, washing does not regenerate activated carbon. Once the pore structure is loaded with adsorbed gas it stays loaded, so odour performance declines steadily regardless of how often you rinse it. Treat it as a pre-filter that happens to have some carbon rather than a genuine odour solution. The PlasmaWave function is an ioniser and it defaults to on. It produces a small amount of ozone, and there is no good reason to run it in a room where someone is sleeping. Switch it off, note that it resets when unplugged, and the unit is a good particulate filter at an excellent price.",
  },
  {
    id: "medify-ma-112",
    name: "Medify MA-112",
    category: "Medical Grade H13 HEPA, Carbon, 2500 sq ft",
    badge: "Best for Whole Floors",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6700,
    pros: [
      "Very high throughput, rated for a genuinely large area",
      "H13 grade HEPA media, a step above standard specification",
      "Dual filter design increases both capacity and filter life",
      "Effective on heavy smoke events where lesser units are overwhelmed",
      "Lifetime warranty with registered filter purchases",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Physically large and heavy, difficult to move between rooms",
      "Loud at the speeds needed to use its rated capacity",
      "Carbon layer is modest relative to the airflow it processes",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Raw throughput for an open floor plan. The right answer when the room is simply too big for a normal unit.",
    description:
      "There is a category of room that defeats ordinary purifiers entirely: a modern open plan ground floor where kitchen, dining and living space share one volume of several thousand cubic feet. A unit rated for 400 square feet placed in that space does something, but not enough to matter during an actual smoke event, and the readout never comes down. The MA-112 is built for that problem, with dual filters and a fan sized to move enough air to actually turn over a large volume within a useful timeframe. In our large-space test it dropped PM2.5 across an open plan area faster than anything except the Coway, and it sustained that rate because the dual filter arrangement means less pressure drop and slower loading. The H13 media is a genuine step above the standard HEPA grade, capturing a higher fraction of the smallest particles. The trade-offs come from the same physics that make it effective. Moving that much air is loud, and using the machine at the speeds that justify its rating is not compatible with watching television in the same room. It is large and heavy enough that it lives where you put it. And the carbon, while present, is modest relative to the sheer volume of air passing through it, so odour removal fades faster than particulate performance does.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Air Purifier for Smoke",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-air-purifier-for-smoke/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best air purifier for smoke?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Austin Air HealthMate Plus, because it carries fifteen pounds of activated carbon and zeolite rather than the few ounces typical of consumer units, and that is what removes smoke odour rather than just the haze. Coway Airmega 400 is the pick for fast particulate clearance in a large room.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does a HEPA filter remove smoke smell?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. HEPA is a mechanical filter that traps particles, and smoke odour is volatile organic compounds in gas form, which pass straight through. Removing the smell requires activated carbon, and the amount of carbon is what determines how long it keeps working.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What size air purifier do I need for smoke?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Aim for four to five air changes per hour, which usually means buying a unit rated for roughly double your actual room size. Coverage figures on the box typically assume two air changes, which is adequate for dust and not for smoke. Sizing up also lets you run it on a quieter setting.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can you wash and reuse a carbon filter?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Washing removes surface dust and restores airflow, which is worth doing, but it does not regenerate the carbon. Once the microscopic pores are loaded with adsorbed gas they stay loaded, so odour removal does not recover. A saturated carbon filter needs replacing.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should I run an air purifier during wildfire smoke?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Continuously for the duration of the event, and ideally starting before indoor levels rise. Outdoor smoke keeps entering through normal air leakage, so a purifier switched on intermittently spends its time catching up. Close internal doors and treat one or two rooms properly rather than the whole house.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do air purifiers with ionisers work better on smoke?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No, and you should generally switch it off. Ionisers add little to particulate capture in a machine that already has HEPA, and they generate small amounts of ozone, which is a respiratory irritant. That is the opposite of what you want in a room where someone with asthma is sleeping.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "air purifier for smoke",
      "wildfire smoke",
      "HEPA filter",
      "activated carbon",
      "PM2.5",
      "indoor air quality",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Air Purifiers for Smoke</span> in 2026
