        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.2k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#carbon-vs-hepa" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Why Carbon Matters More</a>
          <a href="#smoke-types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Types of Smoke</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#cadr-and-sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing and CADR</a>
          <a href="#running-costs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Filters and Running Costs</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Placement Tips</a>
          <a href="#health" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Health and PM2.5</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Smoke is two problems in one machine. The visible haze is fine particulate, which any decent HEPA filter captures, and the smell is volatile organic compounds in gas form, which HEPA does absolutely nothing about. That distinction explains why so many well-reviewed purifiers clear the air visually and leave a room still smelling like a campfire.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: Why Carbon Matters More Than HEPA for Smoke -->
        <div id="carbon-vs-hepa" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Carbon Matters More Than HEPA for Smoke
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every purifier sold today has a competent HEPA filter, which makes HEPA a poor way to choose between them for smoke. The differentiator is what handles the gas phase.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">HEPA Captures Particles, Nothing Else</h3>

          <p>
            A HEPA filter is a physical mesh. It traps solid particulate down to a fraction of a micron with high efficiency, which handles the visible haze and the fine particulate that damages lungs. It has no mechanism whatsoever for capturing a gas molecule, which passes straight through.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Smell Is Gas, Not Particles</h3>

          <p>
            What your nose registers as smoke is volatile organic compounds — aldehydes, phenols, and dozens of others released by combustion. They are individual molecules in the air, far smaller than any filter fibre spacing, and only adsorption removes them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated Carbon Adsorbs by Surface Area</h3>

          <p>
            Activated carbon is riddled with microscopic pores giving an enormous internal surface area, and gas molecules stick to that surface. The capacity of a carbon filter is therefore a direct function of how much carbon there is, which makes weight the specification that predicts performance.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Most Consumer Purifiers Carry Almost None</h3>

          <p>
            The typical carbon stage is a fabric mat impregnated with carbon dust, weighing a few ounces. It works for a short time and then it is saturated, at which point the machine keeps clearing particulate while the room continues to smell exactly as it did.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Carbon Cannot Be Regenerated at Home</h3>

          <p>
            Once the pores are loaded, they stay loaded. Washing a carbon filter removes surface dust and restores airflow, which is worth doing, but it does not recover adsorption capacity. A saturated carbon filter needs replacing, not cleaning.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Carbon Weight Is the Number</p>
