        <!-- SECTION 2: Wildfire, Cooking and Tobacco Smoke Are Different -->
        <div id="smoke-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Wildfire, Cooking and Tobacco Smoke Are Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            Smoke is a general word covering several quite different mixtures, and the right machine depends on which one you are fighting.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wildfire smoke is mostly fine particulate.</strong> It arrives from outdoors in episodes lasting days, and the priority is high throughput to keep indoor levels down while the event lasts. Carbon helps with the smell, but clean air delivery rate is what protects your lungs.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Cooking smoke is heavily oily.</strong> Scorched oil produces both particulate and a greasy aerosol that coats filters and shortens their life dramatically. A washable pre-filter matters more here than anywhere else, and range hood ventilation does more than any purifier.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Tobacco smoke is the hardest case.</strong> It is a continuous source rather than an episode, and its odour compounds are persistent and adsorb into soft furnishings. Only a large carbon bed makes a meaningful difference, and even then the room needs cleaning as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wood stove and fireplace smoke sits between the two.</strong> Episodic like wildfire but generated indoors, often with a strong creosote odour. A moderate carbon bed handles it if the stove is otherwise drawing properly; if it is not, fix the draw rather than filtering the result.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Match the machine to the source.</strong> A high throughput unit with modest carbon is right for wildfire season. A heavy carbon unit with moderate throughput is right for a smoker or a wood stove. Buying the wrong one produces a machine that runs constantly and never solves the problem.
          </p>
        </div>

