        <!-- SECTION 4: CADR, Room Size and Air Changes -->
        <div id="cadr-and-sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. CADR, Room Size and Air Changes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Purifier marketing quotes a room size in square feet, which is close to meaningless on its own. The number that matters is how many times per hour the machine can turn over the air in your actual space.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Clean air delivery rate is the honest specification.</strong> It states how much filtered air the unit produces per minute, tested independently. A quoted room coverage figure is derived from it, usually at a generous assumption about how many air changes are acceptable.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smoke needs four to five air changes per hour.</strong> The coverage figures printed on boxes commonly assume two, which is adequate for pollen and dust and not adequate for smoke. In practice that means buying a unit rated for roughly double the room you intend to use it in.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Ceiling height is ignored by square footage.</strong> A room with a vaulted ceiling can hold half again as much air as its floor area suggests. Calculate the volume rather than the area if your ceilings are not standard height.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Doors and open plans change everything.</strong> A purifier in an open plan space is treating the whole connected volume, not the corner it stands in. Closing a bedroom door and running a modest unit inside is far more effective than running a large one in an open hallway.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The maximum speed is what the rating assumes.</strong> Coverage figures are measured at full fan speed, which is usually too loud to live with. If you plan to run the unit on medium, size for medium, which typically means going a size up.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Size Up, Then Run It Slower</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A purifier rated for twice your room size, running on medium, moves the same air as a correctly sized one running flat out — and it does so at a fraction of the noise. This is the single most useful buying rule in the category, and it is why people who buy exactly to spec end up switching the machine off because it is too loud.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Filter Life and What It Actually Costs -->
        <div id="running-costs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Filter Life and What It Actually Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The purchase price of an air purifier is often the smaller half of what it costs over five years, and smoke shortens filter life more than any other pollutant.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Smoke loads filters fast.</strong> Manufacturer filter life estimates assume ordinary household dust. A wildfire season or a household with a smoker can halve those intervals, and a saturated filter restricts airflow, which reduces clean air delivery rate long before anyone thinks to check.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Carbon fails silently and first.</strong> The HEPA stage tells you it is loading because airflow drops and the machine gets louder. Carbon gives no such signal: it simply stops adsorbing, and the room starts smelling again while the display still reads green.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A washable pre-filter is worth real money.</strong> The pre-filter catches hair, lint and larger particles that would otherwise clog the expensive HEPA media. Vacuuming it monthly can extend main filter life by a third, and it takes two minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Compare five year costs, not sticker prices.</strong> A cheap unit with expensive proprietary filters replaced twice a year can cost more over five years than a premium machine with multi-year media. Work out the arithmetic before deciding which is expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Filter change indicators are timers, not sensors.</strong> Almost all of them count hours of operation rather than measuring anything. They are a reminder, not a diagnosis, and in a smoky environment they will run late.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy filters from the manufacturer.</strong> Third party HEPA replacements are frequently made to a looser specification and fit poorly enough that air bypasses the media around the edges, which defeats the machine entirely. The saving is not worth it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Placement and Getting the Most From It -->
        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Placement and Getting the Most From It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Where the machine stands and how the room is managed change its effective performance more than most people expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Give it room to breathe.</strong> Purifiers pull air in from one or more faces and exhaust from another. Pushed into a corner or against a sofa, a substantial fraction of the intake is recirculated exhaust, and the effective clearance rate falls sharply.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Close the room off during a smoke event.</strong> A purifier can only clean the volume it is connected to. During wildfire smoke, shutting internal doors and treating one or two rooms properly gives far better results than trying to treat a whole open house.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Seal the obvious leaks.</strong> Outdoor smoke arrives through gaps around windows and doors and through trickle vents. Weatherstripping and a rolled towel at a door threshold reduce the load on the purifier more cheaply than any upgrade.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Run it before you need it.</strong> During a wildfire event, starting the machine early and running it continuously keeps indoor levels low. Switching it on once the room already smells means playing catch-up against a volume that is already loaded.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use the extractor fan when cooking.</strong> A range hood venting outside removes cooking smoke at the source, which is something no purifier can compete with. The purifier handles what escapes, not the plume itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Do not run the ioniser.</strong> Several units include an ionising function that produces small quantities of ozone, a respiratory irritant. It adds little to particulate performance and is the wrong thing to have running in a bedroom, particularly if anyone has asthma.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: What Smoke Actually Does Indoors -->
        <div id="health" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Smoke Actually Does Indoors
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reason to filter smoke is not comfort, and understanding the mechanism helps in deciding how much machine to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>PM2.5 is the fraction that reaches deep into the lungs.</strong> Particles under 2.5 microns are small enough to bypass the upper airway defences and reach the alveoli, where the finest of them cross into the bloodstream. This is why fine particulate is regulated and coarse dust largely is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Indoor levels track outdoor levels closely.</strong> During a wildfire event, an ordinary house reaches a substantial fraction of the outdoor concentration within hours, because normal air leakage brings it in. Closing windows slows this considerably but does not stop it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Children and older adults are affected disproportionately.</strong> Higher breathing rates relative to body size, and reduced respiratory reserve at the other end of life, mean the same exposure lands harder. A purifier in a child's bedroom is the highest value placement in most houses.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Short exposures still matter.</strong> Effects on people with asthma and cardiovascular conditions appear within days of elevated exposure rather than requiring years. Treating a smoke event as worth acting on immediately is medically reasonable rather than excessive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>A cheap meter changes behaviour.</strong> A consumer particulate monitor costs very little and turns an invisible problem into a number. Most people who buy one discover their indoor air is worse than they assumed, usually during cooking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: Frequently Asked Questions -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best air purifier for smoke?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Austin Air HealthMate Plus, because it carries fifteen pounds of activated carbon and zeolite rather than the few ounces typical of consumer units, and that is what removes smoke odour rather than just the haze. Coway Airmega 400 is the pick for fast particulate clearance in a large room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a HEPA filter remove smoke smell?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. HEPA is a mechanical filter that traps particles, and smoke odour is volatile organic compounds in gas form, which pass straight through. Removing the smell requires activated carbon, and the amount of carbon is what determines how long it keeps working.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size air purifier do I need for smoke?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Aim for four to five air changes per hour, which usually means buying a unit rated for roughly double your actual room size. Coverage figures on the box typically assume two air changes, which is adequate for dust and not for smoke. Sizing up also lets you run it on a quieter setting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you wash and reuse a carbon filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Washing removes surface dust and restores airflow, which is worth doing, but it does not regenerate the carbon. Once the microscopic pores are loaded with adsorbed gas they stay loaded, so odour removal does not recover. A saturated carbon filter needs replacing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should I run an air purifier during wildfire smoke?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Continuously for the duration of the event, and ideally starting before indoor levels rise. Outdoor smoke keeps entering through normal air leakage, so a purifier switched on intermittently spends its time catching up. Close internal doors and treat one or two rooms properly rather than the whole house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do air purifiers with ionisers work better on smoke?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, and you should generally switch it off. Ionisers add little to particulate capture in a machine that already has HEPA, and they generate small amounts of ozone, which is a respiratory irritant. That is the opposite of what you want in a room where someone with asthma is sleeping.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Air Purifier Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Smoke exposes the difference between a purifier that looks good on paper and one that solves the problem, and the mistakes are consistent.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on HEPA Rating Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Almost every purifier sold has competent HEPA media, which makes it useless as a way to choose between them for smoke. The differentiating specification is carbon weight, and it is frequently absent from the marketing entirely. If a manufacturer will not state how much carbon is in the machine, assume the answer is a few ounces.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the Box Rating</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Coverage figures assume around two air changes per hour, which handles dust and pollen. Smoke needs four to five, so a unit sized exactly to your room will be running flat out and still losing. Buy for roughly double the area, which also lets you run it on a quieter setting.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the Ioniser Switched On</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Several units default to running an ionising function that produces small amounts of ozone, a known respiratory irritant. It contributes very little to particulate capture in a machine that already has a HEPA filter, and it is the last thing that should be running in a bedroom during a smoke event.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Pre-Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The pre-filter catches hair, lint and coarse particles that would otherwise clog the expensive HEPA media, and vacuuming it monthly can extend main filter life by a third. Skipping it means paying for premature filter replacements while the machine quietly loses airflow and clean air delivery rate.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

